<h1>Video 097 — java.util.concurrent.locks (ReentrantLock)</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: Multithreading Enhancement  Part- 3|| java.util.concurrent.locks</p>
<p>ASR decode: concurrent.loss / loocks = locks; re-entrant / reenter / reentrance clock = ReentrantLock; holes / hold / personal count = hold count; religious lock = releases lock; fitness / fail / fair = fairness; ceued / Q = queued; get a Q length = getQueueLength; get ceued threads = getQueuedThreads; has ceued threads = hasQueuedThreads; is a fair = isFair; trilock / tryck = tryLock; synchronizer = synchronized; dhi / da / don / dhoni = Dhoni; your / yoras / yuvi / jas = Yuvi/Yuvraj; good morning jar = Good Morning; keep subing = keep writing; legend = listen.</p>
<h3>00:05 — Recap: Lock interface five methods</h3>
<p>Last session: Lock interface in java.util.concurrent.locks (1.5) because of synchronized problems (performance, deadlock, missing API).</p>
<p>Five methods of Lock:</p>
<ol><li>lock()</li></ol>
<ol><li>tryLock()</li></ol>
<ol><li>tryLock with time period</li></ol>
<ol><li>lockInterruptibly()</li></ol>
<ol><li>unlock()</li></ol>
<h3>01:43 — Implementation class: ReentrantLock</h3>
<p>Cannot do anything with the interface alone — need implementation. `ReentrantLock` implements Lock. Direct child of `Object`.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

// Board:
// ReentrantLock is the implementation class of Lock interface
// and it is the direct child class of Object</pre>
<h3>02:12 — Why the word “reentrant”?</h3>
<p>Not namesake. Meaning: again and again.</p>
<p>Demo on board: same thread can call lock() multiple times on same lock object; JVM maintains a hold count.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock();

l.lock(); // hold count = 1 (was 0)
l.lock(); // hold count = 2
l.lock(); // hold count = 3

l.unlock(); // hold count = 2  (lock NOT released yet)
l.unlock(); // hold count = 1
l.unlock(); // hold count = 0 → lock released</pre>
<p>Same thread can hold multiple holds on the same lock. Lock is released only when hold count reaches zero. One unlock with hold count 3 does not release the lock.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// Reentrant means a thread can acquire same lock multiple times
// without any issue
// Internally ReentrantLock increments thread&#x27;s hold count
// whenever we call lock()
// and decrements count value whenever thread calls unlock()
// and the lock will be released whenever count reaches zero</pre>
<h3>09:07 — Constructors of ReentrantLock</h3>
<p>1. No-arg:</p>
<p>ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock();
// creates an instance of ReentrantLock</p>
<p>2. With fairness:</p>
<p>ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock(boolean fairness);
// creates a ReentrantLock with the given fairness policy</p>
<p>Fairness = “be a bit fair / don’t be biased.” Allowed values: true / false.</p>
<ul><li>If fairness = true → longest waiting thread can acquire the lock when available → first-come-first-serve (FCFS) policy.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If fairness = false → which waiting thread gets the chance? We can’t expect.</li></ul>
<p>Default fairness (no-arg constructor): `false`.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// If fairness is true → longest waiting thread can acquire the lock
//   if it is available (follows first come first serve policy)
// If fairness is false → which waiting thread will get the chance
//   we can&#x27;t expect
// Default value for fairness is false</pre>
<h3>15:34 — Which declarations are equal?</h3>
<p>ReentrantLock l1 = new ReentrantLock();
ReentrantLock l2 = new ReentrantLock(true);
ReentrantLock l3 = new ReentrantLock(false);
// all the above?</p>
<p>First and third are equal (default fairness is false).</p>
<h3>17:20 — Important methods of ReentrantLock</h3>
<p>Because ReentrantLock implements Lock, it has the five Lock methods:</p>
<pre class='code'>void lock();
boolean tryLock();
boolean tryLock(long time, TimeUnit unit) throws InterruptedException;
void lockInterruptibly() throws InterruptedException;
void unlock();</pre>
<p>Plus extra ReentrantLock methods:</p>
<pre class='code'>int getHoldCount();
boolean isHeldByCurrentThread();
int getQueueLength();
java.util.Collection&lt;Thread&gt; getQueuedThreads();
boolean hasQueuedThreads();
boolean isLocked();
boolean isFair();
Thread getOwner();</pre>
<h3>21:56 — Board meanings of extra methods</h3>
<pre class='code'>// getHoldCount()
// returns number of holds on this lock by current thread

// isHeldByCurrentThread()
// returns true if and only if lock is held by current thread

// getQueueLength()
// returns number of threads waiting for the lock

// getQueuedThreads()
// returns a Collection of threads which are waiting to get the lock

// hasQueuedThreads()
// returns true if any thread waiting to get the lock

// isLocked()
// returns true if the lock is acquired by some thread
// (same thread or another)

// isFair()
// returns true if the fairness policy is set with true value

// getOwner()
// returns the Thread which acquires the lock</pre>
<h3>28:08 — Example 1: ReentrantLockDemo2 (method familiarity)</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class ReentrantLockDemo2 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock();

        l.lock();
        l.lock();
        System.out.println(l.isLocked());              // prints true
        System.out.println(l.isHeldByCurrentThread()); // prints true
        System.out.println(l.getQueueLength());        // prints 0
        System.out.println(l.getHoldCount());          // prints 2

        l.unlock();
        System.out.println(l.getHoldCount());          // prints 1
        System.out.println(l.isLocked());              // prints true

        l.unlock();
        System.out.println(l.isLocked());              // prints false
        System.out.println(l.isFair());                // prints false
    }
}</pre>
<p>He compiles/runs: output sequence discussed as true true 2 0 1 true false (hold count 2 before first unlock; queue length 0). Beside each SOP, corresponding output on board.</p>
<h3>36:48 — Example 2: replace synchronized with ReentrantLock (wish / Good Morning)</h3>
<p>Classic multithreading sync demo: Display.wish(String name) prints Good Morning then sleeps 2s then prints name, loop 10 times. Two threads on same Display with names Dhoni and Yuvi → without sync = irregular/mixed output; with synchronized wish = regular output (one name completes before other).</p>
<p>Without sync (irregular):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Display {
    public void wish(String name) {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.print(&quot;Good Morning: &quot;);
            try {
                Thread.sleep(2000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            }
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}

class MyThread extends Thread {
    Display d;
    String name;

    MyThread(Display d, String name) {
        this.d = d;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void run() {
        d.wish(name);
    }
}

class SynchronizedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Display d = new Display();
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(d, &quot;Dhoni&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(d, &quot;Yuvi&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        // prints mixed / irregular &quot;Good Morning&quot; lines
    }
}</pre>
<p>With synchronized method → regular:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Display {
    public synchronized void wish(String name) {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.print(&quot;Good Morning: &quot;);
            try {
                Thread.sleep(2000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            }
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}
// prints regular: all Good Morning Dhoni then all Good Morning Yuvi (order of who wins first may vary)</pre>
<h3>48:01 — Same example with ReentrantLock instead of synchronized</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class Display {
    ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock();

    public void wish(String name) {
        l.lock(); // line 1
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
                System.out.print(&quot;Good Morning: &quot;);
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(2000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                }
                System.out.println(name);
            }
        } finally {
            l.unlock(); // line 2
        }
    }
}

class MyThread extends Thread {
    Display d;
    String name;

    MyThread(Display d, String name) {
        this.d = d;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void run() {
        d.wish(name);
    }
}

class ReentrantLockDemo1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Display d = new Display();
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(d, &quot;Dhoni&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(d, &quot;Yuvi&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        // prints regular output (one thread at a time inside wish)
    }
}</pre>
<p>Flow: Dhoni gets l.lock(), Yuvi waits; after Dhoni unlock, Yuvi proceeds. Same guarantee as synchronized for this example — but ReentrantLock is more powerful (tryLock etc. next).</p>
<h3>59:51 — Comment line 1 and line 2</h3>
<pre class='code'>// If we comment line 1 (lock) and line 2 (unlock):
//   threads execute simultaneously → irregular output

// If we are not commenting lines 1 and 2:
//   threads execute one by one → regular output</pre>
<h3>1:02:24 — Flexibility: tryLock without / with time</h3>
<p>Synchronized cannot “try without waiting” or “wait max N time.” ReentrantLock can.</p>
<h3>1:02:42 — Demo 3: `tryLock()` (ReentrantLockDemo3)</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class MyThread extends Thread {
    static ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock(); // one lock for all threads

    MyThread(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    public void run() {
        if (l.tryLock()) {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + &quot; got lock and performing safe operations&quot;);
            try {
                Thread.sleep(2000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            }
            l.unlock();
        } else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + &quot; unable to get lock and hence performing alternative operations&quot;);
        }
    }
}

class ReentrantLockDemo3 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(&quot;First Thread&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(&quot;Second Thread&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Typical run he shows:</p>
<pre class='code'>// prints First Thread got lock and performing safe operations
// prints Second Thread unable to get lock and hence performing alternative operations</pre>
<p>(No guarantee which thread wins; with short code often first thread. Second never waits — tryLock returns false → else.)</p>
<p>static lock: only one lock object even for multiple MyThread instances.</p>
<p>Not possible with traditional synchronized: unavailable lock ⇒ wait until get.</p>
<h3>1:16:54 — Demo 4: `tryLock` with time + do-while retry (ReentrantLockDemo4)</h3>
<p>Special care: do-while infinite until break.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class MyThread extends Thread {
    static ReentrantLock l = new ReentrantLock();

    MyThread(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    public void run() {
        do {
            try {
                if (l.tryLock(5000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + &quot; got lock&quot;);
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(30000); // 30 seconds
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    }
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + &quot; releases lock&quot;);
                    l.unlock();
                    break;
                } else {
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                            + &quot; unable to get lock and will try again&quot;);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            }
        } while (true);
    }
}

class ReentrantLockDemo4 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(&quot;First Thread&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(&quot;Second Thread&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Behavior he narrates:</p>
<ul><li>First thread gets lock, sleeps 30s.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Second waits max 5s per tryLock; fails → “unable to get lock and will try again”; repeats (~6 times over 30s).</li></ul>
<ul><li>When first releases, second gets lock, sleeps 30s, unlocks, breaks.</li></ul>
<p>Imports: java.util.concurrent.locks.* and java.util.concurrent.* because TimeUnit is in concurrent package.</p>
<h3>1:35:47 — Closing: prefer Lock over synchronized</h3>
<p>After knowing Lock / ReentrantLock, usage of synchronized is never recommended — more flexibility (tryLock with/without time; deadlock less relevant with tryLock). Most people still habit-use synchronized only.</p>
